    <description />
    <content:encoded>&lt;!-- wp:image {"id":3909} --&gt;
&lt;figure class="wp-block-image"&gt;&lt;img src="https://flyingsummers.com/wp-content/uploads/2017/01/smo-n972rd.jpg" alt="smo-n972rd.jpg" class="wp-image-3909" /&gt;&lt;/figure&gt;
&lt;!-- /wp:image --&gt;

&lt;!-- wp:paragraph --&gt;
&lt;p&gt;N972RD is a 2007 Diamond Twinstar (DA42) that we purchased in March 2016. It has two Centurion  engines burning JetA, three-bladed MT propellers, and a &lt;a rel="noopener" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Garmin_G1000" target="_blank"&gt;Garmin G1000 avionics suite&lt;/a&gt;. It is for sale and available on September 1, 2023, since we are flying it for the summer.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;!-- /wp:paragraph --&gt;

&lt;!-- wp:paragraph --&gt;
&lt;p&gt;This plane is an odd duck in terms of hours because one of the engines was replaced under warranty (the block warped) when the plane had 900 hours. So when we took delivery, one of the engines had 950 hours and the other had just 50 hours.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;!-- /wp:paragraph --&gt;

&lt;!-- wp:paragraph --&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://flyingsummers.com/2016/12/20/bringing-it-all-back-home/"&gt;In October 2016&lt;/a&gt; it was brought to the Diamond factory in London, Ontario, where during three months they did these major upgrades:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;!-- /wp:paragraph --&gt;

&lt;!-- wp:list --&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;&lt;!-- wp:list-item --&gt;
&lt;li&gt;A &lt;a href="https://flyingsummers.com/2016/09/06/otto-youre-fired/"&gt;new autopilot&lt;/a&gt;, replacing the KAP 140 with the Garmin GFC-700; this is a lot of work since it requires replacing the servos and gives you a constant-airspeed climb (via the FLC button).&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;!-- /wp:list-item --&gt;

&lt;!-- wp:list-item --&gt;
&lt;li&gt;ADS-B in and out, via Garmin's GTX 345 transponder.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;!-- /wp:list-item --&gt;

&lt;!-- wp:list-item --&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;a href="https://flyingsummers.com/2010/05/18/waas/"&gt;WAAS radios&lt;/a&gt; for the GPS receivers.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;!-- /wp:list-item --&gt;

&lt;!-- wp:list-item --&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Those three upgrades meant that the box for the LRU's, in the baggage area, was also upgraded. By mistake, they ran all of the new wiring necessary for a re-engining of the plane to Austro engines. We didn't sign up for new engines, so they didn't charge us, but apparently one of the more painful parts of that work has already been completed.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;!-- /wp:list-item --&gt;

&lt;!-- wp:list-item --&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;a href="https://flyingsummers.com/2016/09/11/no-co-pilot-needed/"&gt;Reclining seats in the front&lt;/a&gt;, a huge improvement for long flights.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;!-- /wp:list-item --&gt;

&lt;!-- wp:list-item --&gt;
&lt;li&gt;An all new interior, critical since it was a flight club plane and had seen some long cross country flights and use by people not invested in keeping the leather nice.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;!-- /wp:list-item --&gt;

&lt;!-- wp:list-item --&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Installation of &lt;a href="https://flyingsummers.com/2016/09/07/where-are-dee-batteries/"&gt;powered headset ports&lt;/a&gt;. &lt;/li&gt;
&lt;!-- /wp:list-item --&gt;

&lt;!-- wp:list-item --&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Powered USB ports, one by the pilot's left knee, one in the instrument panel, and two in the luggage area perfect for powering devices the rear seat passengers are using.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;!-- /wp:list-item --&gt;

&lt;!-- wp:list-item --&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Removal of &lt;a href="https://flyingsummers.com/2016/09/11/no-co-pilot-needed/"&gt;the right seat's control stick&lt;/a&gt;, allowing the right seat passenger to work on a laptop during long flights. (Obviously aided by the USB power port in the instrument panel right in front of the co-pilot's seat.)&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;!-- /wp:list-item --&gt;&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;!-- /wp:list --&gt;

&lt;!-- wp:paragraph --&gt;
&lt;p&gt;From the week we took delivery, we have always continued to nudge the plane along toward being the perfect machine to take us into the sky and across the country. This has included some simple things, including:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;!-- /wp:paragraph --&gt;

&lt;!-- wp:list --&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;&lt;!-- wp:list-item --&gt;
&lt;li&gt;April 2016 a &lt;a href="https://flyingsummers.com/2016/07/08/cover-up/"&gt;proper cover&lt;/a&gt; and engine cowl plugs by &lt;a href="https://aircraftcovers.com/" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer noopener"&gt;Bruce's Covers&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;!-- /wp:list-item --&gt;

&lt;!-- wp:list-item --&gt;
&lt;li&gt;May 2016 a &lt;a href="https://flyingsummers.com/2016/03/24/hints-for-your-aircraft/"&gt;circuit breaker cover&lt;/a&gt; to quickly identify the breaker for the autopilot&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;!-- /wp:list-item --&gt;

&lt;!-- wp:list-item --&gt;
&lt;li&gt;July 2018 &lt;a href="https://flyingsummers.com/2018/08/02/stop-flying/"&gt;much better brakes&lt;/a&gt; by &lt;a rel="noreferrer noopener" href="http://www.beringer-aero.com/en/" target="_blank"&gt;Beringer&lt;/a&gt; &lt;/li&gt;
&lt;!-- /wp:list-item --&gt;

&lt;!-- wp:list-item --&gt;
&lt;li&gt;April 2022 the &lt;a href="https://flyingsummers.com/2022/04/16/constant-improvements/"&gt;addition of inspection ports&lt;/a&gt; to check the pushrod rollers.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;!-- /wp:list-item --&gt;&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;!-- /wp:list --&gt;

&lt;!-- wp:paragraph --&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The plane has been cared for by &lt;a href="http://flyingsummers.com/2016/07/04/one-more-engine-and-a-team-of-mechanics/"&gt;excellent mechanics&lt;/a&gt;. It has always been serviced at Diamond Service Centers, except in the rare instances when we have been AOG at an airport without one (a tiny wire broke on the starter in Cleveland KBKL, for instance, and we had a flat tire over in Burbank KBUR). Although David Seastead (currently working with Premier in Fort Worth, Texas) has been responsible for the plane since we took ownership, we have also had Glenn Lawler at SouthTec Aviation do two annuals, one when we purchased it and one when we had an ECU replaced. Galvin Flying at KBFI Boeing Field, Seattle, Washington has done some good work on the plane. All three of those shops have very deep Diamond Aircraft knowledge.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;!-- /wp:paragraph --&gt;

&lt;!-- wp:paragraph --&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The annual will be fresh as of June 1 2023.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;!-- /wp:paragraph --&gt;

